1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,970
In the 1870s, a photographer
carried his camera

2
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:06,739
into an empty house on the edge of Madrid.

3
00:00:07,219 --> 00:00:08,959
The plaster was cracking.

4
00:00:08,979 --> 00:00:10,983
The house was falling apart.

5
00:00:11,463 --> 00:00:13,123
And on the walls…

6
00:00:13,143 --> 00:00:15,183
someone had painted nightmares.

7
00:00:15,923 --> 00:00:18,063
A giant, eating a man.

8
00:00:18,083 --> 00:00:20,623
Witches, gathered around a goat.

9
00:00:20,643 --> 00:00:25,223
Two old people grinning over a bowl
of soup, like corpses at dinner.

10
00:00:25,963 --> 00:00:28,583
Nobody was supposed to see these.

11
00:00:28,603 --> 00:00:30,263
They had no titles.

12
00:00:30,283 --> 00:00:31,623
No signature.

13
00:00:31,643 --> 00:00:35,567
The man who painted them never
wrote a single word about them.

14
00:00:36,177 --> 00:00:38,717
He was the most famous painter in Spain.

15
00:00:38,737 --> 00:00:40,927
Francisco de Goya.

16
00:00:41,057 --> 00:00:43,622
And these were the walls of his own home.

17
00:00:44,492 --> 00:00:47,272
This is the story of the Black Paintings.

18
00:00:47,292 --> 00:00:50,415
Why he painted them, and how they escaped.

19
00:00:52,675 --> 00:00:53,695
Madrid.

20
00:00:53,715 --> 00:00:55,592
February, 1819.

21
00:00:56,278 --> 00:00:58,498
Goya is seventy-two years old.

22
00:00:58,518 --> 00:01:02,738
He has painted kings, queens and
dukes for more than thirty years.

23
00:01:02,758 --> 00:01:04,738
He is rich. He is famous.

24
00:01:04,758 --> 00:01:07,746
And he hasn't heard a
sound in twenty-six years.

25
00:01:08,356 --> 00:01:11,456
An illness took his hearing
when he was forty-six.

26
00:01:11,476 --> 00:01:16,363
Since then, he has lived through a war
with Napoleon, and painted what he saw.

27
00:01:17,103 --> 00:01:21,643
Now he buys a house outside the
city, across the Manzanares river.

28
00:01:21,663 --> 00:01:24,063
A farmhouse with two large rooms.

29
00:01:24,673 --> 00:01:27,053
The neighbours already have a name for it.

30
00:01:27,073 --> 00:01:29,053
La Quinta del Sordo.

31
00:01:29,073 --> 00:01:30,704
The Deaf Man's House.

32
00:01:31,344 --> 00:01:32,924
Not because of Goya.

33
00:01:32,944 --> 00:01:35,703
The man who owned it
before him was deaf, too.

34
00:01:36,443 --> 00:01:41,223
Goya moves in with Leocadia Weiss, a
woman more than forty years younger,

35
00:01:41,243 --> 00:01:45,433
his housekeeper and companion,
and her little daughter, Rosario.

36
00:01:46,054 --> 00:01:47,234
Why here?

37
00:01:47,254 --> 00:01:49,794
Historians think he wanted to get away.

38
00:01:49,814 --> 00:01:53,874
From the court, and from the
king, Ferdinand the Seventh.

39
00:01:55,774 --> 00:01:58,932
At the end of that year, Goya nearly dies.

40
00:01:59,412 --> 00:02:01,392
We don't know what the illness was.

41
00:02:01,412 --> 00:02:03,392
We know how close it came…

42
00:02:03,412 --> 00:02:05,023
because he painted it.

43
00:02:05,633 --> 00:02:09,133
A doctor holds him up and
puts a glass to his lips.

44
00:02:09,153 --> 00:02:11,930
Behind them, faces wait in the dark.

45
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:15,400
At the bottom, Goya
painted a thank-you note.

46
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:21,160
To his friend, Doctor Arrieta, for the
skill and care with which he saved his life,

47
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:25,530
in his acute and dangerous
illness, at the end of 1819,

48
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:27,753
at seventy-three years of age.

49
00:02:28,493 --> 00:02:29,963
He survives.

50
00:02:30,013 --> 00:02:33,513
And when he can stand again,
he picks up his brushes…

51
00:02:33,533 --> 00:02:35,290
and starts on the walls.

52
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:39,410
The rooms were already decorated.

53
00:02:39,430 --> 00:02:41,010
Cheerful country scenes.

54
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:42,998
Little figures in a landscape.

55
00:02:43,348 --> 00:02:45,248
Goya paints over all of it.

56
00:02:45,268 --> 00:02:47,565
In oil, straight onto the plaster.

57
00:02:48,175 --> 00:02:50,475
Downstairs is the dining room.

58
00:02:50,495 --> 00:02:54,715
One wall holds a crowd of pilgrims,
stumbling home through the dark,

59
00:02:54,735 --> 00:02:56,395
mouths wide open.

60
00:02:56,415 --> 00:02:58,235
Singing, or screaming.

61
00:02:58,255 --> 00:03:02,390
Painted by a man who hadn't heard
a voice in almost thirty years.

62
00:03:03,130 --> 00:03:07,270
On the other long wall, witches
gather around a huge black goat.

63
00:03:07,290 --> 00:03:09,449
The Devil, preaching in the dark.

64
00:03:10,189 --> 00:03:14,329
By the door, a woman in black
leans on what looks like a grave.

65
00:03:14,349 --> 00:03:16,398
Many think it's Leocadia.

66
00:03:17,008 --> 00:03:18,348
Two old men.

67
00:03:18,368 --> 00:03:22,472
One shouts into the other's ear,
the way you'd talk to a deaf man.

68
00:03:23,082 --> 00:03:25,839
Judith, about to cut off a general's head.

69
00:03:26,449 --> 00:03:31,096
And on the back wall, facing
the woman in black, is Saturn.

70
00:03:32,356 --> 00:03:34,496
Upstairs, there is a second room.

71
00:03:34,516 --> 00:03:38,256
Two men club each other, sunk
to their knees in the earth.

72
00:03:38,276 --> 00:03:41,776
The Fates float over the
land, one holding scissors.

73
00:03:41,796 --> 00:03:45,536
Two figures fly over a rock
while soldiers take aim.

74
00:03:45,556 --> 00:03:46,656
Men reading.

75
00:03:46,676 --> 00:03:47,936
Women laughing.

76
00:03:47,956 --> 00:03:50,306
A procession of the Inquisition.

77
00:03:50,596 --> 00:03:52,176
And by the door…

78
00:03:52,196 --> 00:03:53,586
a dog.

79
00:03:53,716 --> 00:03:55,494
We'll come back to the dog.

80
00:03:56,414 --> 00:03:58,184
Fourteen paintings.

81
00:03:58,204 --> 00:03:59,544
No titles.

82
00:03:59,564 --> 00:04:00,954
No signatures.

83
00:04:01,084 --> 00:04:02,344
No dates.

84
00:04:02,364 --> 00:04:06,664
Every name you know them by was
given later, by other people.

85
00:04:06,684 --> 00:04:09,117
Today we call them the Black Paintings.

86
00:04:11,017 --> 00:04:14,197
In the old legend, the
titan Saturn is warned

87
00:04:14,217 --> 00:04:16,997
that one of his children
will overthrow him.

88
00:04:17,017 --> 00:04:20,182
So, as each one is born, he swallows it.

89
00:04:20,792 --> 00:04:25,492
Rubens painted the scene for the King of
Spain, almost two hundred years earlier.

90
00:04:25,512 --> 00:04:28,469
Goya could have seen it
in the royal collection.

91
00:04:28,949 --> 00:04:32,129
In Rubens, Saturn is a god doing his job.

92
00:04:32,149 --> 00:04:33,089
Calm.

93
00:04:33,109 --> 00:04:34,359
Calculating.

94
00:04:34,988 --> 00:04:37,158
Goya's Saturn is something else.

95
00:04:37,178 --> 00:04:38,838
Look at his eyes.

96
00:04:38,858 --> 00:04:40,598
He doesn't look powerful.

97
00:04:40,618 --> 00:04:42,248
He looks terrified.

98
00:04:42,378 --> 00:04:44,861
Like someone who can't
stop what he's doing.

99
00:04:45,601 --> 00:04:47,341
And look at who he's eating.

100
00:04:47,361 --> 00:04:50,461
It isn't swallowed. It's being torn apart.

101
00:04:50,481 --> 00:04:52,461
The head is already gone.

102
00:04:52,481 --> 00:04:54,381
And it doesn't look like a baby.

103
00:04:54,401 --> 00:04:56,752
One scholar argues it's an adult.

104
00:04:57,492 --> 00:04:59,392
Here's the strange part.

105
00:04:59,412 --> 00:05:02,592
Even those eyes aren't entirely by Goya.

106
00:05:02,612 --> 00:05:06,700
They were so badly damaged that
a restorer had to repaint them.

107
00:05:07,180 --> 00:05:12,520
And some historians claim the original wall
showed something else, something sexual,

108
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:15,311
that was painted out
when the walls came down.

109
00:05:16,051 --> 00:05:17,471
What does it mean?

110
00:05:17,491 --> 00:05:19,391
Time devouring everything.

111
00:05:19,411 --> 00:05:21,151
Old age eating youth.

112
00:05:21,171 --> 00:05:23,871
Spain devouring its own children in war.

113
00:05:23,891 --> 00:05:26,031
A man afraid of going mad.

114
00:05:26,051 --> 00:05:28,358
Every one of these has been argued.

115
00:05:28,378 --> 00:05:29,785
Goya never said.

116
00:05:30,265 --> 00:05:32,325
All we know is where he put it.

117
00:05:32,345 --> 00:05:34,009
In the room where he ate.

118
00:05:35,909 --> 00:05:39,649
In 1823, a French army marches into Spain

119
00:05:39,669 --> 00:05:42,985
and hands King Ferdinand
back his absolute power.

120
00:05:43,335 --> 00:05:47,205
Liberals are arrested, exiled, executed.

121
00:05:47,495 --> 00:05:49,950
And Goya has friends on the wrong side.

122
00:05:50,430 --> 00:05:54,540
That year, he signs the house
over to his grandson, Mariano.

123
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:58,572
Then he goes into hiding, at
the home of a friend, a priest.

124
00:05:59,182 --> 00:06:03,242
In May 1824, he asks the
king for permission to leave,

125
00:06:03,262 --> 00:06:05,737
to take the waters at a spa in France.

126
00:06:06,217 --> 00:06:07,717
The king says yes.

127
00:06:07,737 --> 00:06:13,465
Goya crosses the border at seventy-eight,
deaf, and alone, and settles in Bordeaux.

128
00:06:14,075 --> 00:06:16,615
He comes back to Madrid only briefly.

129
00:06:16,635 --> 00:06:22,021
On the sixteenth of April, 1828,
he dies in Bordeaux, at eighty-two.

130
00:06:22,631 --> 00:06:27,571
He leaves fourteen paintings on the
walls of a house that isn't his anymore.

131
00:06:27,591 --> 00:06:31,074
And in all his letters,
not one word about them.

132
00:06:32,974 --> 00:06:36,554
After his death, a painter
friend, Antonio Brugada,

133
00:06:36,574 --> 00:06:39,514
walks through the house and
lists what's on the walls.

134
00:06:39,534 --> 00:06:42,874
Forty years later, a French
writer does the same.

135
00:06:42,894 --> 00:06:46,201
Those two lists are how we
know where each painting hung.

136
00:06:46,811 --> 00:06:50,151
The house passes through the
family, then to strangers.

137
00:06:50,171 --> 00:06:52,704
Fifty years on, it's falling apart.

138
00:06:53,314 --> 00:06:57,214
In 1873, a rich banker from Paris buys it.

139
00:06:57,234 --> 00:06:59,154
Baron Émile d'Erlanger.

140
00:06:59,634 --> 00:07:04,814
Before anything is touched, a photographer,
Jean Laurent, records the walls.

141
00:07:04,834 --> 00:07:08,094
That's the photographer from
the beginning of this story.

142
00:07:08,114 --> 00:07:12,941
His pictures are the closest we will
ever get to what Goya actually painted.

143
00:07:13,681 --> 00:07:18,781
Then the Baron hires the Prado's chief
restorer, Salvador Martínez Cubells,

144
00:07:18,801 --> 00:07:21,047
to take the paintings off the walls.

145
00:07:21,527 --> 00:07:26,867
Piece by piece, the paint is lifted
off the plaster and glued onto canvas.

146
00:07:26,887 --> 00:07:29,507
What breaks is painted back in.

147
00:07:29,527 --> 00:07:33,238
Experts agree that much of what
we see today was repainted.

148
00:07:33,848 --> 00:07:38,468
The witches were even cut down,
probably to fit a wall in Paris.

149
00:07:38,488 --> 00:07:40,148
Because that was the plan.

150
00:07:40,168 --> 00:07:41,188
Paris.

151
00:07:41,208 --> 00:07:43,478
The World's Fair of 1878.

152
00:07:44,258 --> 00:07:47,838
They hang in the Trocadéro
Palace from May to November.

153
00:07:47,858 --> 00:07:49,358
They don't sell.

154
00:07:49,378 --> 00:07:52,158
The next year, a British critic calls Goya

155
00:07:52,178 --> 00:07:55,910
a monster of immorality,
impudence, and ingratitude.

156
00:07:56,650 --> 00:07:59,990
In 1881, the Baron gives them to Spain.

157
00:08:00,010 --> 00:08:01,830
They go to the Prado.

158
00:08:01,850 --> 00:08:03,830
And the Deaf Man's House?

159
00:08:03,850 --> 00:08:06,040
Demolished, in 1909.

160
00:08:08,065 --> 00:08:12,765
In 2003, a Spanish art
historian, Juan José Junquera,

161
00:08:12,785 --> 00:08:14,685
makes an explosive claim.

162
00:08:14,705 --> 00:08:16,487
Goya didn't paint them.

163
00:08:17,227 --> 00:08:22,247
His evidence: property records that
describe the house with only one floor.

164
00:08:22,267 --> 00:08:26,986
If the upstairs room didn't exist in
Goya's time, who painted its walls?

165
00:08:27,726 --> 00:08:30,666
His suspect: Goya's own son, Javier.

166
00:08:30,686 --> 00:08:33,386
And the grandson, Mariano, short of money,

167
00:08:33,406 --> 00:08:35,940
would have sold them as his grandfather's.

168
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:38,500
Most experts reject it.

169
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:43,510
The British Goya scholar Nigel Glendinning
said he was totally unconvinced.

170
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:46,260
The Prado still hangs them as Goya's.

171
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:48,340
But the doubt says something true.

172
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:52,260
We have no letter, no title, no witness.

173
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:53,571
Just the walls.

174
00:08:55,091 --> 00:08:57,079
Which brings us back to the dog.

175
00:08:57,559 --> 00:09:00,979
A small dog, sunk up to
its neck in something.

176
00:09:00,999 --> 00:09:03,219
Sand, or mud. We don't know.

177
00:09:03,239 --> 00:09:05,857
Looking up into an enormous empty space.

178
00:09:06,467 --> 00:09:07,887
No legend.

179
00:09:07,907 --> 00:09:09,167
No story.

180
00:09:09,187 --> 00:09:11,620
Just a dog, and nothing.

181
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:17,236
The Spanish painter Antonio Saura called
it the most beautiful picture in the world.

182
00:09:17,976 --> 00:09:20,756
Maybe it's the one Goya understood best.

183
00:09:20,776 --> 00:09:21,716
Old.

184
00:09:21,736 --> 00:09:22,756
Deaf.

185
00:09:22,776 --> 00:09:25,853
Looking up at something
no one else could see.

186
00:09:26,933 --> 00:09:30,693
Goya painted it on the wall
of his house, for no one.

187
00:09:31,563 --> 00:09:33,583
It hangs in the Prado, in Madrid.
